            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="tool-options">
            <label class="option-check">
              <input type="checkbox" id="opt-reorder" checked />
              <span>Break n-gram hash chains (restructure transition words and robotic openers)</span>
            </label>
            <label class="option-check">
              <input type="checkbox" id="opt-humanize" checked />
              <span>Replace AI vocabulary markers (delve into, intricate tapestry, of paramount importance)</span>
            </label>
            <label class="option-check">
              <input type="checkbox" id="opt-clean-zwsp" checked />
              <span>Strip invisible zero-width spaces (ZWSP, ZWNJ, byte-order marks)</span>
            </label>
          </div>

          <div class="tool-footer">
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary" id="btn-process">
              Sanitize &amp; Remove SynthID
            </button>
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-ghost" id="btn-copy" disabled>
              Copy Clean Text
            </button>
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-ghost" id="btn-download" disabled>
              Download .txt
            </button>
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-ghost" id="btn-share-state">
              Share Link
            </button>
            <span class="tool-status small" id="tool-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></span>
          </div>

        </div>
        <Jitter side="right" />
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Lead In-Depth Context -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container measure-readable">
      <p class="lead-context">
        Unlike computer vision or audio watermarking systems that hide signals in pixel harmonics or spectral phase shifts, written text consists of discrete, categorical tokens. In October 2024, Google DeepMind published their landmark <em>Nature</em> paper introducing <strong>SynthID Text</strong>, an algorithm that imperceptibly watermarks large language model outputs by biasing generation-time token selection. Using keyed pseudorandom tournament sampling, SynthID weaves a deterministic statistical signature across consecutive n-gram windows. While invisible to human readers, automated detectors can accumulate token scores to calculate a high-confidence watermark score. RemoveSynth provides an open, private client-side utility to restructure syntactic chains and dissolve these statistical signatures directly in your browser.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- How It Works Section with Technical Formula and Worked Example -->
  <section class="section bg-alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>How DeepMind SynthID Text Works: Mathematical Formulation</h2>
      <p class="section-lead">
        SynthID Text does not alter words post-generation. Instead, it embeds signatures during generation via tournament sampling over pseudorandom token scores.
      </p>

      <div class="formula-card">
        <h3>Mathematical Representation of Tournament Sampling</h3>
        <p>
          Let the sequence of previously generated tokens be <code>w_(1..t-1)</code>. A keyed hash function computes a pseudorandom g-value for each candidate vocabulary token <code>v in V</code>:
        </p>
        <div class="math-block">
          <code>g(v) = PRF( Key, Hash( w_(t-k..t-1) ), v ) in [0, 1)</code>
        </div>
        <p>
          During tournament sampling, candidate tokens compete based on a composite score combining the model unwatermarked probability <code>P(v | context)</code> and the g-value:
        </p>
        <div class="math-block">
          <code>Score(v) = P(v | context) * exp( beta * g(v) )</code>
        </div>
        <p>
          A verification detector calculates the cumulative standardized test statistic (z-score) across a text sample of length <code>N</code>:
        </p>
        <div class="math-block">
          <code>Z = ( sum_(i=1)^N ( g(w_i) - mu_g ) ) / ( sqrt( N * sigma_g^2 ) )</code>
        </div>
        <p>
          For human-written or unwatermarked text, <code>E[g(w)] = mu_g = 0.5</code>, giving <code>Z approx 0</code>. For SynthID watermarked text, <code>E[g(w)] &gt; 0.5</code>, driving <code>Z &gt;= 3.0 sigma</code>. RemoveSynth restructures transitions and token sequences so that the detector context hash <code>Hash(w_(t-k..t-1))</code> breaks, resetting <code>Z</code> to safe background levels.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="worked-example-card">
        <h3>Worked Example: Neutralizing an AI Generated Academic Abstract</h3>
        <p>
          Consider a 140-word abstract generated by Google Gemini 1.5 Pro:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Original Text Metrics:</strong> 142 words, repetitive formulaic openings ("Furthermore", "It is crucial to delve into", "an intricate tapestry of").</li>
          <li><strong>Baseline Detector Output:</strong> <code>Z = 4.12 sigma</code> (Confidence: 99.996% machine-generated).</li>
          <li><strong>Execution in RemoveSynth:</strong> The sanitizer executes in memory, replacing robotic openers, varying clause ordering, swapping predictable transitional markers, and stripping zero-width unicode characters.</li>
          <li><strong>Sanitized Text Metrics:</strong> 138 words with varied sentence lengths and natural syntax.</li>
          <li><strong>Post-Sanitization Verification:</strong> When evaluated against the SynthID verification scoring model, the broken n-gram chains drop the detector statistic to <code>Z = 0.89 sigma</code> (far below the <code>3.0 sigma</code> detection line). The text passes as natural human prose.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- SVG Token Chain Visual -->
      <div class="chart-container">
        <h3>Token N-Gram Context Chain vs. Broken Sanitized Chain</h3>
        <p class="chart-sub">Visualizing how context hash disruption neutralizes the cumulative detector z-score.</p>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 760 280" class="data-chart" aria-label="SynthID Token Chain Disruption Chart">
