  <!-- Sub-Search In-Depth Content -->
  <section class="section bg-alt">
    <div class="container measure-readable">
      <h2>Deep-Dive: SynthID Text Architecture &amp; Detection Countermeasures</h2>

      <div class="subsearch-block">
        <h3>How to Detect SynthID Text</h3>
        <p>
          Detection requires access to the secret key <code>Key</code> and the tournament scoring pseudorandom function used by Google DeepMind. The detector iterates over tokens, reconstructing the n-gram context window for each position. If the accumulated score produces a z-score above 3.0 sigma, the text is flagged. Without the private key, third-party detectors rely on perplexity proxies and known AI syntactic patterns.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="subsearch-block">
        <h3>Gemma Open-Weights Watermarking Explained</h3>
        <p>
          Google released <code>synthid-text</code> as an open-source Python library on GitHub and Hugging Face for integration into Gemma models. Developers deploying local Gemma instances can enable SynthID during text generation via custom logits processors. RemoveSynth effectively sanitizes outputs from both proprietary Gemini endpoints and self-hosted Gemma instances.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="subsearch-block">
        <h3>Zero-Width Space (ZWSP) Removal</h3>
        <p>
          Certain watermarking systems insert non-printing unicode characters like Zero-Width Space (U+200B), Zero-Width Non-Joiner (U+200C), and Byte Order Marks (U+FEFF) into prose. These characters form invisible binary payloads that survive copy-pasting. RemoveSynth strips all invisible unicode code points instantly.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="subsearch-block">
        <h3>Why Paraphrasing and Restructuring Neutralizes SynthID</h3>
        <p>
          Because SynthID relies strictly on forward sequential hashing of n-gram tokens, altering even one word in a sequence changes the input hash for all subsequent tokens in that window. Breaking up long compound sentences, varying transition phrasing, and inverting clauses dissolves the mathematical chain completely.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="subsearch-block">
        <h3>Minimum Token Threshold for Watermark Detection</h3>
        <p>
          Statistical confidence scales with sample size <code>N</code>. On passages under 50 words, random variation produces high variance in z-scores, making detection statistically unreliable. On longer passages exceeding 200 words, watermarks become readily detectable unless syntactic restructuring is applied.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

